#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# siegel_theta

#% text
# `siegel_theta(omega, s)` gives the Siegel (Riemann) theta function `sum exp(pi i n.omega.n + 2 pi i n.s)` over the integer vectors `n`; `siegel_theta([nu1, nu2], omega, s)` gives the function with characteristics.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `omega` is a symmetric matrix with a positive definite imaginary part; other matrices stay unevaluated.

#% item
# With characteristics the terms are `exp(pi i (n + nu1).omega.(n + nu1) + 2 pi i (n + nu1).(s + nu2))`.

#% item
# The sum runs over the lattice points where the terms reach the working precision, around the largest one; the omitted terms are bounded and added to the radius.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
siegel_theta([[im, -1/2], [-1/2, im]], [0.5, 0])
#% output
#= 1.0074837203450846

#% cell
n(siegel_theta([[im, 0], [0, im]], [1, 2]), 30)
#% output
#= 1.1803405990160962260453379405584886

#% cell
siegel_theta([[1/2, 0], [0, 1/2]], [[im, 0], [0, im]], [0.1 + im, 0])
#% output
#= 14.849894009124256 - 10.789079534000802im

#% text
# In one dimension it is a Jacobi theta function:

#% cell
siegel_theta([[0.5 + im]], [2])
#% output
#= 1.0000069746847124 + 0.08642783652859559im

#% cell
elliptic_theta(3, 2*pi, exp(im*pi*(0.5 + im)))
#% output
#= 1.0000069746847124 + 0.08642783652859562im

#% text
# A matrix that is not symmetric:

#% cell
siegel_theta([[im, 1], [0, 2*im]], [1, 2.0])
#% output
#= siegel_theta([[im, 1], [0, 2im]], [1, 2.0])

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `elliptic_theta`, `riemann_siegel_theta`
